{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FaLN6x83inZjPAYLANPi2L","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd009-du\n     2→title: \"cmd/du: Recursive Directory Disk Usage\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  du reports disk usage for directories and files, but macOS BSD du and GNU du diverge in\n     6→  default block size (512-byte vs 1024-byte blocks), flag availability (-d vs --max-depth,\n     7→  --apparent-size absent in BSD du), and output formatting. Scripts that parse du output\n     8→  break when moving between platforms. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU\n     9→  reference binary (gdu, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all in-scope flag\n    10→  combinations, verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n    11→\n    12→  du requires pkg/sys for extended file metadata (st_blocks for physical block counts,\n    13→  st_dev and st_ino for hard-link deduplication) and pkg/format for human-readable size\n    14→  conversion (-h flag). These shared components are specified in prd002-sys and\n    15→  prd003-format respectively.\n    16→\n    17→goals:\n    18→  - G1: Report disk usage for directories by recursively accumulating block counts, matching GNU du default output.\n    19→  - G2: Support summary mode (-s) to print only the total for each argument.\n    20→  - G3: Support human-readable output (-h) matching GNU du -h formatting.\n    21→  - G4: Support all-files mode (-a) to report sizes for individual files, not just directories.\n    22→  - G5: Support depth limiting (-d N / --max-depth=N) to control recursion depth.\n    23→  - G6: Support grand total (-c) to print a cumulative total after all arguments.\n    24→  - G7: Support apparent size (--apparent-size) to report file size instead of block count.\n    25→\n    26→requirements:\n    27→  R1:\n    28→    title: Default behavior\n    29→    items:\n    30→      - R1.1: Must recurse into each directory argument and print the accumulated size and path for each subdirectory and the directory itself. When no arguments are given, must default to the current directory (\".\").\n    31→      - R1.2: |\n    32→          Must report sizes in 1024-byte (1K) block units by default. Each entry's size is fi.Blocks / 2 where fi.Blocks (int64) is the number of 512-byte blocks allocated (st_blocks from syscall.Stat_t). Obtain metadata via pkg/sys.Stat(path string) (*sys.FileInfo, error) — follows symbolic links. sys.FileInfo.Blocks int64 holds the 512-byte block count.\n    33→\n    34→          sys.FileInfo struct (embedded for reference):\n    35→            type FileInfo struct {\n    36→                Mode    os.FileMode\n    37→                Size    int64       // apparent file size in bytes (st_size)\n    38→                Nlink   uint64      // hard-link count (st_nlink)\n    39→                Uid     uint32      // owner user ID (st_uid)\n    40→                Gid     uint32      // owner group ID (st_gid)\n    41→                Rdev    uint64      // device ID for special files (st_rdev)\n    42→                Dev     uint64      // device ID of the containing filesystem (st_dev)\n    43→                Ino     uint64      // inode number (st_ino)\n    44→                Blocks  int64       // 512-byte blocks allocated (st_blocks)\n    45→                Blksize int64       // preferred I/O block size\n    46→                ModTime time.Time   // modification time\n    47→                Sys     os.FileInfo // underlying os.FileInfo\n    48→            }\n    49→      - R1.3: Must print one line per entry in the format \"SIZE\\tPATH\\n\" where SIZE is a right-aligned integer (no leading spaces) and PATH is the relative path as given by the directory traversal.\n    50→      - R1.4: Must not follow symbolic links during traversal. Use pkg/sys.Lstat(path string) (*sys.FileInfo, error) — does not follow symbolic links; returns the symlink's own metadata. pkg/sys.Lstat has the same return type as pkg/sys.Stat (see R1.2 for sys.FileInfo definition).\n    51→      - R1.5: Must process multiple directory arguments in the order given on the command line. Each argument is traversed independently.\n    52→\n    53→  R2:\n    54→    title: Flag behavior\n    55→    items:\n    56→      - R2.1: |\n    57→          -h must display sizes as human-readable strings using binary mode (1024-based) via pkg/format.HumanSize(bytes int64, opts HumanSizeOpts) string with opts.Binary = true (1024-base units, suffixes K/M/G/T/P/E). -h applies to all output lines including the grand total line when -c is also given.\n    58→      - R2.2: -s must print only one line per argument with the total size of that argument. When -s is given, subdirectory lines must not be printed. -s is equivalent to --max-depth=0.\n    59→      - R2.3: -a must print a size line for every file encountered during traversal, not just directories. Without -a, only directory entries are printed (the default).\n    60→      - R2.4: -d N (and the long form --max-depth=N) must limit the depth of reported entries. Depth 0 means only the argument itself (equivalent to -s). Depth 1 means the argument and its immediate children. Files and directories deeper than N are accumulated into their parent's total but not printed as separate lines.\n    61→      - R2.5: -k must report sizes in 1024-byte blocks. Since 1K is already the default, -k has no visible effect but must be accepted without error for compatibility.\n    62→      - R2.6: -m must report sizes in 1048576-byte (1M) blocks. Each entry's st_blocks value is converted from 512-byte blocks to 1M blocks, rounding up.\n    63→      - R2.7: -c must print a grand total line after all arguments are processed. The grand total line has the format \"SIZE\\ttotal\\n\" where SIZE is the sum of all argument totals.\n    64→      - R2.8: --apparent-size must report file sizes using st_size (the apparent file size in bytes) instead of st_blocks (the physical block allocation). The apparent size is then converted to the selected block unit (1K by default, or as overridden by -k, -m, or -h). Obtains st_size from the os.FileInfo.Size() method.\n    65→\n    66→  R3:\n    67→    title: Hard-link deduplication\n    68→    items:\n    69→      - R3.1: Must track files by their device number (st_dev) and inode number (st_ino) across the entire du invocation. A file that has already been counted (same st_dev and st_ino) must not be counted again. This prevents double-counting hard-linked files, matching GNU du behavior (du.c:554 inode tracking via hash table).\n    70→      - R3.2: Must obtain dev and ino via pkg/sys.Stat or pkg/sys.Lstat (see R1.2 for sys.FileInfo). Use sys.FileInfo.Dev uint64 (device ID of the containing filesystem, st_dev) and sys.FileInfo.Ino uint64 (inode number, st_ino) as the deduplication key. The map is keyed by a struct{ Dev, Ino uint64 } pair.\n    71→      - R3.3: Hard-link deduplication applies per du invocation, not per argument. A hard-linked file counted under one argument must not be counted again under a subsequent argument.\n    72→\n    73→  R4:\n    74→    title: Exit codes\n    75→    items:\n    76→      - R4.1: Must exit 0 when all arguments are traversed and reported successfully.\n    77→      - R4.2: Must exit 1 when any error occurs during traversal (cannot stat a file, cannot read a directory, permission denied). Must print a diagnostic to stderr for each error and continue processing remaining entries and arguments before exiting.\n    78→\n    79→  R5:\n    80→    title: Signal handling\n    81→    items:\n    82→      - R5.1: Must install a SIGPIPE handler at startup by calling pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() so that piping du output to head or other truncating consumers does not produce a broken-pipe error message. InstallSIGPIPEHandler uses signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel; a goroutine calls os.Exit(0) on receipt. Must not use signal.Ignore.\n    83→\n    84→non_goals:\n    85→  - --si (1000-based units) is out of scope; we support only binary (1024-based) human-readable output.\n    86→  - --exclude and --exclude-from pattern-based exclusion filters are out of scope.\n    87→  - -D / --dereference-args (dereference only command-line symlink arguments) is out of scope.\n    88→  - -L / --dereference (dereference all symlinks during traversal) is out of scope.\n    89→  - -l / --count-links (count hard-linked files multiple times) is out of scope.\n    90→  - --time (show modification time alongside size) is out of scope.\n    91→  - --inodes (count inodes instead of block usage) is out of scope.\n    92→  - -b / --bytes (equivalent to --apparent-size --block-size=1) is out of scope as a shorthand; --apparent-size with default 1K blocks is sufficient.\n    93→  - --block-size=SIZE with arbitrary unit suffixes is out of scope; only -k and -m are supported.\n    94→  - -0 / --null (NUL-terminated output) is out of scope.\n    95→\n    96→acceptance_criteria:\n    97→  - \"AC1: Default mode comparison -- gdu and go run ./cmd/du on a test directory fixture produce identical output. Differential tests must use -k to normalize block size and run both binaries on the same directory on the same filesystem.\"\n    98→  - \"AC2: Summary mode -- gdu -s and go run ./cmd/du -s produce identical single-line output per argument on the same fixture.\"\n    99→  - \"AC3: Human-readable sizes -- gdu -h and go run ./cmd/du -h produce identical size strings on a fixture with directories of known cumulative sizes.\"\n   100→  - \"AC4: Hard-link deduplication -- a fixture containing one file hard-linked twice in different subdirectories produces the same total with gdu and the Go binary. The total must count the file's blocks only once.\"\n   101→  - \"AC5: Apparent size -- gdu --apparent-size and go run ./cmd/du --apparent-size produce identical output on a fixture containing a sparse file (where apparent size differs from block allocation).\"\n   102→  - \"AC6: Depth limiting -- gdu -d 1 and go run ./cmd/du -d 1 produce identical output, showing only the top-level entries and the argument total.\"\n   103→  - \"AC7: Grand total -- gdu -c dir1 dir2 and go run ./cmd/du -c dir1 dir2 produce identical output including the \\\"total\\\" line.\"\n   104→  - \"AC8: Exit codes -- the Go binary exits 1 when given a non-existent directory argument and prints a diagnostic to stderr, matching gdu behavior.\"\n   105→  - \"AC9: cmd/du compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n   106→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"afee3052-baa4-4d89-84b3-61d45e69f953","uuid":"dad6b6d8-1394-4b87-aa1c-86fbb5050659","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/239/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd009-du.yaml","content":"id: prd009-du\ntitle: \"cmd/du: Recursive Directory Disk Usage\"\n\nproblem: |\n  du reports disk usage for directories and files, but macOS BSD du and GNU du diverge in\n  default block size (512-byte vs 1024-byte blocks), flag availability (-d vs --max-depth,\n  --apparent-size absent in BSD du), and output formatting. Scripts that parse du output\n  break when moving between platforms. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU\n  reference binary (gdu, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all in-scope flag\n  combinations, verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n\n  du requires pkg/sys for extended file metadata (st_blocks for physical block counts,\n  st_dev and st_ino for hard-link deduplication) and pkg/format for human-readable size\n  conversion (-h flag). These shared components are specified in prd002-sys and\n  prd003-format respectively.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Report disk usage for directories by recursively accumulating block counts, matching GNU du default output.\n  - G2: Support summary mode (-s) to print only the total for each argument.\n  - G3: Support human-readable output (-h) matching GNU du -h formatting.\n  - G4: Support all-files mode (-a) to report sizes for individual files, not just directories.\n  - G5: Support depth limiting (-d N / --max-depth=N) to control recursion depth.\n  - G6: Support grand total (-c) to print a cumulative total after all arguments.\n  - G7: Support apparent size (--apparent-size) to report file size instead of block count.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Default behavior\n    items:\n      - R1.1: Must recurse into each directory argument and print the accumulated size and path for each subdirectory and the directory itself. When no arguments are given, must default to the current directory (\".\").\n      - R1.2: |\n          Must report sizes in 1024-byte (1K) block units by default. Each entry's size is fi.Blocks / 2 where fi.Blocks (int64) is the number of 512-byte blocks allocated (st_blocks from syscall.Stat_t). Obtain metadata via pkg/sys.Stat(path string) (*sys.FileInfo, error) — follows symbolic links. sys.FileInfo.Blocks int64 holds the 512-byte block count.\n\n          sys.FileInfo struct (embedded for reference):\n            type FileInfo struct {\n                Mode    os.FileMode\n                Size    int64       // apparent file size in bytes (st_size)\n                Nlink   uint64      // hard-link count (st_nlink)\n                Uid     uint32      // owner user ID (st_uid)\n                Gid     uint32      // owner group ID (st_gid)\n                Rdev    uint64      // device ID for special files (st_rdev)\n                Dev     uint64      // device ID of the containing filesystem (st_dev)\n                Ino     uint64      // inode number (st_ino)\n                Blocks  int64       // 512-byte blocks allocated (st_blocks)\n                Blksize int64       // preferred I/O block size\n                ModTime time.Time   // modification time\n                Sys     os.FileInfo // underlying os.FileInfo\n            }\n      - R1.3: Must print one line per entry in the format \"SIZE\\tPATH\\n\" where SIZE is a right-aligned integer (no leading spaces) and PATH is the relative path as given by the directory traversal.\n      - R1.4: Must not follow symbolic links during traversal. Use pkg/sys.Lstat(path string) (*sys.FileInfo, error) — does not follow symbolic links; returns the symlink's own metadata. pkg/sys.Lstat has the same return type as pkg/sys.Stat (see R1.2 for sys.FileInfo definition).\n      - R1.5: Must process multiple directory arguments in the order given on the command line. Each argument is traversed independently.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Flag behavior\n    items:\n      - R2.1: |\n          -h must display sizes as human-readable strings using binary mode (1024-based) via pkg/format.HumanSize(bytes int64, opts HumanSizeOpts) string with opts.Binary = true (1024-base units, suffixes K/M/G/T/P/E). -h applies to all output lines including the grand total line when -c is also given.\n      - R2.2: -s must print only one line per argument with the total size of that argument. When -s is given, subdirectory lines must not be printed. -s is equivalent to --max-depth=0.\n      - R2.3: -a must print a size line for every file encountered during traversal, not just directories. Without -a, only directory entries are printed (the default).\n      - R2.4: -d N (and the long form --max-depth=N) must limit the depth of reported entries. Depth 0 means only the argument itself (equivalent to -s). Depth 1 means the argument and its immediate children. Files and directories deeper than N are accumulated into their parent's total but not printed as separate lines.\n      - R2.5: -k must report sizes in 1024-byte blocks. Since 1K is already the default, -k has no visible effect but must be accepted without error for compatibility.\n      - R2.6: -m must report sizes in 1048576-byte (1M) blocks. Each entry's st_blocks value is converted from 512-byte blocks to 1M blocks, rounding up.\n      - R2.7: -c must print a grand total line after all arguments are processed. The grand total line has the format \"SIZE\\ttotal\\n\" where SIZE is the sum of all argument totals.\n      - R2.8: --apparent-size must report file sizes using st_size (the apparent file size in bytes) instead of st_blocks (the physical block allocation). The apparent size is then converted to the selected block unit (1K by default, or as overridden by -k, -m, or -h). Obtains st_size from the os.FileInfo.Size() method.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Hard-link deduplication\n    items:\n      - R3.1: Must track files by their device number (st_dev) and inode number (st_ino) across the entire du invocation. A file that has already been counted (same st_dev and st_ino) must not be counted again. This prevents double-counting hard-linked files, matching GNU du behavior (du.c:554 inode tracking via hash table).\n      - R3.2: Must obtain dev and ino via pkg/sys.Stat or pkg/sys.Lstat (see R1.2 for sys.FileInfo). Use sys.FileInfo.Dev uint64 (device ID of the containing filesystem, st_dev) and sys.FileInfo.Ino uint64 (inode number, st_ino) as the deduplication key. The map is keyed by a struct{ Dev, Ino uint64 } pair.\n      - R3.3: Hard-link deduplication applies per du invocation, not per argument. A hard-linked file counted under one argument must not be counted again under a subsequent argument.\n\n  R4:\n    title: Exit codes\n    items:\n      - R4.1: Must exit 0 when all arguments are traversed and reported successfully.\n      - R4.2: Must exit 1 when any error occurs during traversal (cannot stat a file, cannot read a directory, permission denied). Must print a diagnostic to stderr for each error and continue processing remaining entries and arguments before exiting.\n\n  R5:\n    title: Signal handling\n    items:\n      - R5.1: Must install a SIGPIPE handler at startup by calling pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() so that piping du output to head or other truncating consumers does not produce a broken-pipe error message. InstallSIGPIPEHandler uses signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel; a goroutine calls os.Exit(0) on receipt. Must not use signal.Ignore.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - --si (1000-based units) is out of scope; we support only binary (1024-based) human-readable output.\n  - --exclude and --exclude-from pattern-based exclusion filters are out of scope.\n  - -D / --dereference-args (dereference only command-line symlink arguments) is out of scope.\n  - -L / --dereference (dereference all symlinks during traversal) is out of scope.\n  - -l / --count-links (count hard-linked files multiple times) is out of scope.\n  - --time (show modification time alongside size) is out of scope.\n  - --inodes (count inodes instead of block usage) is out of scope.\n  - -b / --bytes (equivalent to --apparent-size --block-size=1) is out of scope as a shorthand; --apparent-size with default 1K blocks is sufficient.\n  - --block-size=SIZE with arbitrary unit suffixes is out of scope; only -k and -m are supported.\n  - -0 / --null (NUL-terminated output) is out of scope.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: Default mode comparison -- gdu and go run ./cmd/du on a test directory fixture produce identical output. Differential tests must use -k to normalize block size and run both binaries on the same directory on the same filesystem.\"\n  - \"AC2: Summary mode -- gdu -s and go run ./cmd/du -s produce identical single-line output per argument on the same fixture.\"\n  - \"AC3: Human-readable sizes -- gdu -h and go run ./cmd/du -h produce identical size strings on a fixture with directories of known cumulative sizes.\"\n  - \"AC4: Hard-link deduplication -- a fixture containing one file hard-linked twice in different subdirectories produces the same total with gdu and the Go binary. The total must count the file's blocks only once.\"\n  - \"AC5: Apparent size -- gdu --apparent-size and go run ./cmd/du --apparent-size produce identical output on a fixture containing a sparse file (where apparent size differs from block allocation).\"\n  - \"AC6: Depth limiting -- gdu -d 1 and go run ./cmd/du -d 1 produce identical output, showing only the top-level entries and the argument total.\"\n  - \"AC7: Grand total -- gdu -c dir1 dir2 and go run ./cmd/du -c dir1 dir2 produce identical output including the \\\"total\\\" line.\"\n  - \"AC8: Exit codes -- the Go binary exits 1 when given a non-existent directory argument and prints a diagnostic to stderr, matching gdu behavior.\"\n  - \"AC9: cmd/du compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n","numLines":106,"startLine":1,"totalLines":106}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011jHUPUkDBqa3DSsAKtXjno","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→// Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\n     2→// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n     3→\n     4→// Differential tests for du core disk usage reporting.\n     5→//\n     6→// Implements prd009-du R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, R1.5.\n     7→package main\n     8→\n     9→import (\n    10→\t\"bytes\"\n    11→\t\"fmt\"\n    12→\t\"os\"\n    13→\t\"os/exec\"\n    14→\t\"path/filepath\"\n    15→\t\"testing\"\n    16→\n    17→\t\"github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/testutils\"\n    18→)\n    19→\n    20→// goBinary is the path to the compiled Go du binary. Set by TestMain.\n    21→var goBinary string\n    22→\n    23→// refBinary is the path to the GNU gdu reference binary. Set by TestMain.\n    24→var refBinary string\n    25→\n    26→// TestMain builds the Go du binary and locates the gdu reference binary.\n    27→// D1: skip all tests if gdu is not on PATH.\n    28→// D1: build Go du binary into a temporary directory.\n    29→func TestMain(m *testing.M) {\n    30→\tref, err := exec.LookPath(\"gdu\")\n    31→\tif err != nil {\n    32→\t\tfmt.Fprintln(os.Stderr, \"gdu not found on PATH; skipping du differential tests\")\n    33→\t\tos.Exit(0)\n    34→\t}\n    35→\trefBinary = ref\n    36→\n    37→\tbinDir, err := os.MkdirTemp(\"\", \"du-test-*\")\n    38→\tif err != nil {\n    39→\t\tfmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, \"creating bin dir: %v\\n\", err)\n    40→\t\tos.Exit(1)\n    41→\t}\n    42→\n    43→\tgoBinary = filepath.Join(binDir, \"du\")\n    44→\tcmd := exec.Command(\"go\", \"build\", \"-o\", goBinary, \".\")\n    45→\tif out, err := cmd.CombinedOutput(); err != nil {\n    46→\t\tfmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, \"building Go du binary: %v\\n%s\", err, out)\n    47→\t\tos.RemoveAll(binDir) // best-effort cleanup\n    48→\t\tos.Exit(1)\n    49→\t}\n    50→\n    51→\tcode := m.Run()\n    52→\tos.RemoveAll(binDir) // best-effort cleanup\n    53→\tos.Exit(code)\n    54→}\n    55→\n    56→// writeTestFile creates a file with the given content in dir.\n    57→func writeTestFile(t *testing.T, dir, name, content string) {\n    58→\tt.Helper()\n    59→\tif err := os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(dir, name), []byte(content), 0o644); err != nil {\n    60→\t\tt.Fatalf(\"writing test file %s: %v\", name, err)\n    61→\t}\n    62→}\n    63→\n    64→// normalizeProgramName replaces \"gdu: \" with \"du: \" in output so stderr\n    65→// from the GNU reference binary and the Go binary can be compared.\n    66→func normalizeProgramName(data []byte) []byte {\n    67→\treturn bytes.ReplaceAll(data, []byte(\"gdu: \"), []byte(\"du: \"))\n    68→}\n    69→\n    70→// buildDirTree creates a two-level directory tree for testing recursive\n    71→// traversal. The tree has the structure:\n    72→//\n    73→//\troot/\n    74→//\t  file1.txt  (small file)\n    75→//\t  sub/\n    76→//\t    file2.txt  (small file)\n    77→//\t    deep/\n    78→//\t      file3.txt  (small file)\n    79→//\n    80→// D4: at least two levels of nesting to exercise walkDir recursion.\n    81→func buildDirTree(t *testing.T, root string) {\n    82→\tt.Helper()\n    83→\tsub := filepath.Join(root, \"sub\")\n    84→\tdeep := filepath.Join(sub, \"deep\")\n    85→\tif err := os.MkdirAll(deep, 0o755); err != nil {\n    86→\t\tt.Fatalf(\"creating directory tree: %v\", err)\n    87→\t}\n    88→\twriteTestFile(t, root, \"file1.txt\", \"hello world\\n\")\n    89→\twriteTestFile(t, sub, \"file2.txt\", \"second file content\\n\")\n    90→\twriteTestFile(t, deep, \"file3.txt\", \"deep nested file\\n\")\n    91→}\n    92→\n    93→// TestDuRecursiveTraversal verifies R1.1: du recurses into each directory\n    94→// and prints accumulated size for each subdirectory and the directory itself.\n    95→// Also exercises R1.2 (block unit display) and R1.3 (SIZE\\tPATH format)\n    96→// since RunDiffTests compares stdout byte-for-byte.\n    97→func TestDuRecursiveTraversal(t *testing.T) {\n    98→\tt.Parallel()\n    99→\n   100→\tdir := t.TempDir()\n   101→\tbuildDirTree(t, dir)\n   102→\n   103→\ttestutils.RunDiffTests(t, goBinary, refBinary, []testutils.DiffTest{\n   104→\t\t{\n   105→\t\t\tName:     \"recursive-directory-tree\",\n   106→\t\t\tArgs:     []string{dir},\n   107→\t\t\tExitCode: 0,\n   108→\t\t},\n   109→\t})\n   110→}\n   111→\n   112→// TestDuBlockUnits verifies R1.2: both binaries report identical block counts\n   113→// for the same directory tree, confirming the Go implementation's 512-to-1024\n   114→// block conversion matches the reference binary. The byte-for-byte stdout\n   115→// comparison in RunDiffTests confirms block counts agree.\n   116→func TestDuBlockUnits(t *testing.T) {\n   117→\tt.Parallel()\n   118→\n   119→\tdir := t.TempDir()\n   120→\tbuildDirTree(t, dir)\n   121→\n   122→\t// Run on a subdirectory to verify block counts at a different tree level.\n   123→\tsub := filepath.Join(dir, \"sub\")\n   124→\n   125→\ttestutils.RunDiffTests(t, goBinary, refBinary, []testutils.DiffTest{\n   126→\t\t{\n   127→\t\t\tName:     \"block-units-subdirectory\",\n   128→\t\t\tArgs:     []string{sub},\n   129→\t\t\tExitCode: 0,\n   130→\t\t},\n   131→\t})\n   132→}\n   133→\n   134→// TestDuOutputFormat verifies R1.3: each output line is \"SIZE\\tPATH\\n\" for both\n   135→// directory and non-directory file arguments.\n   136→// D4: tests both directory and non-directory arguments to exercise both branches\n   137→// of duArg.\n   138→func TestDuOutputFormat(t *testing.T) {\n   139→\tt.Parallel()\n   140→\n   141→\tdir := t.TempDir()\n   142→\tbuildDirTree(t, dir)\n   143→\n   144→\tfilePath := filepath.Join(dir, \"file1.txt\")\n   145→\n   146→\ttestutils.RunDiffTests(t, goBinary, refBinary, []testutils.DiffTest{\n   147→\t\t{\n   148→\t\t\tName:     \"format-directory-arg\",\n   149→\t\t\tArgs:     []string{dir},\n   150→\t\t\tExitCode: 0,\n   151→\t\t},\n   152→\t\t{\n   153→\t\t\tName:     \"format-non-directory-arg\",\n   154→\t\t\tArgs:     []string{filePath},\n   155→\t\t\tExitCode: 0,\n   156→\t\t},\n   157→\t})\n   158→}\n   159→\n   160→// TestDuDefaultArgument verifies R1.5: when no arguments are given, du defaults\n   161→// to \".\". Run both binaries with no arguments inside a temporary directory and\n   162→// verify identical output.\n   163→func TestDuDefaultArgument(t *testing.T) {\n   164→\tt.Parallel()\n   165→\n   166→\tdir := t.TempDir()\n   167→\tbuildDirTree(t, dir)\n   168→\n   169→\ttestutils.RunDiffTests(t, goBinary, refBinary, []testutils.DiffTest{\n   170→\t\t{\n   171→\t\t\tName:     \"default-current-directory\",\n   172→\t\t\tWorkDir:  dir,\n   173→\t\t\tExitCode: 0,\n   174→\t\t},\n   175→\t})\n   176→}\n   177→\n   178→// TestDuMultipleArguments verifies R1.5: multiple directory arguments are\n   179→// processed in command-line order, each traversed independently.\n   180→func TestDuMultipleArguments(t *testing.T) {\n   181→\tt.Parallel()\n   182→\n   183→\tdir := t.TempDir()\n   184→\tbuildDirTree(t, dir)\n   185→\n   186→\tsub := filepath.Join(dir, \"sub\")\n   187→\tdeep := filepath.Join(dir, \"sub\", \"deep\")\n   188→\n   189→\ttestutils.RunDiffTests(t, goBinary, refBinary, []testutils.DiffTest{\n   190→\t\t{\n   191→\t\t\tName:     \"multiple-directory-args\",\n   192→\t\t\tArgs:     []string{sub, deep},\n   193→\t\t\tExitCode: 0,\n   194→\t\t},\n   195→\t})\n   196→}\n   197→\n   198→// TestDuNonexistentArgument verifies R4.1 and R4.2: du exits 1 for a\n   199→// nonexistent path and prints a diagnostic to stderr.\n   200→func TestDuNonexistentArgument(t *testing.T) {\n   201→\tt.Parallel()\n   202→\n   203→\tdir := t.TempDir()\n   204→\n   205→\ttestutils.RunDiffTests(t, goBinary, refBinary, []testutils.DiffTest{\n   206→\t\t{\n   207→\t\t\tName:      \"nonexistent-path\",\n   208→\t\t\tArgs:      []string{filepath.Join(dir, \"does-not-exist\")},\n   209→\t\t\tExitCode:  1,\n   210→\t\t\tNormalize: []testutils.NormalizeFunc{normalizeProgramName},\n   211→\t\t},\n   212→\t})\n   213→}\n   214→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"afee3052-baa4-4d89-84b3-61d45e69f953","uuid":"747b9ad3-a4ba-4655-8fd9-6651d226f3b6","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/239/cmd/du/du_test.go","content":"// Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n\n// Differential tests for du core disk usage reporting.\n//\n// Implements prd009-du R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, R1.5.\npackage main\n\nimport (\n\t\"bytes\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"os/exec\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"testing\"\n\n\t\"github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/testutils\"\n)\n\n// goBinary is the path to the compiled Go du binary. Set by TestMain.\nvar goBinary string\n\n// refBinary is the path to the GNU gdu reference binary. Set by TestMain.\nvar refBinary string\n\n// TestMain builds the Go du binary and locates the gdu reference binary.\n// D1: skip all tests if gdu is not on PATH.\n// D1: build Go du binary into a temporary directory.\nfunc TestMain(m *testing.M) {\n\tref, err := exec.LookPath(\"gdu\")\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tfmt.Fprintln(os.Stderr, \"gdu not found on PATH; skipping du differential tests\")\n\t\tos.Exit(0)\n\t}\n\trefBinary = ref\n\n\tbinDir, err := os.MkdirTemp(\"\", \"du-test-*\")\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tfmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, \"creating bin dir: %v\\n\", err)\n\t\tos.Exit(1)\n\t}\n\n\tgoBinary = filepath.Join(binDir, \"du\")\n\tcmd := exec.Command(\"go\", \"build\", \"-o\", goBinary, \".\")\n\tif out, err := cmd.CombinedOutput(); err != nil {\n\t\tfmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, \"building Go du binary: %v\\n%s\", err, out)\n\t\tos.RemoveAll(binDir) // best-effort cleanup\n\t\tos.Exit(1)\n\t}\n\n\tcode := m.Run()\n\tos.RemoveAll(binDir) // best-effort cleanup\n\tos.Exit(code)\n}\n\n// writeTestFile creates a file with the given content in dir.\nfunc writeTestFile(t *testing.T, dir, name, content string) {\n\tt.Helper()\n\tif err := os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(dir, name), []byte(content), 0o644); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"writing test file %s: %v\", name, err)\n\t}\n}\n\n// normalizeProgramName replaces \"gdu: \" with \"du: \" in output so stderr\n// from the GNU reference binary and the Go binary can be compared.\nfunc normalizeProgramName(data []byte) []byte {\n\treturn bytes.ReplaceAll(data, []byte(\"gdu: \"), []byte(\"du: \"))\n}\n\n// buildDirTree creates a two-level directory tree for testing recursive\n// traversal. The tree has the structure:\n//\n//\troot/\n//\t  file1.txt  (small file)\n//\t  sub/\n//\t    file2.txt  (small file)\n//\t    deep/\n//\t      file3.txt  (small file)\n//\n// D4: at least two levels of nesting to exercise walkDir recursion.\nfunc buildDirTree(t *testing.T, root string) {\n\tt.Helper()\n\tsub := filepath.Join(root, \"sub\")\n\tdeep := filepath.Join(sub, \"deep\")\n\tif err := os.MkdirAll(deep, 0o755); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"creating directory tree: %v\", err)\n\t}\n\twriteTestFile(t, root, \"file1.txt\", \"hello world\\n\")\n\twriteTestFile(t, sub, \"file2.txt\", \"second file content\\n\")\n\twriteTestFile(t, deep, \"file3.txt\", \"deep nested file\\n\")\n}\n\n// TestDuRecursiveTraversal verifies R1.1: du recurses into each directory\n// and prints accumulated size for each subdirectory and the directory itself.\n// Also exercises R1.2 (block unit display) and R1.3 (SIZE\\tPATH format)\n// since RunDiffTests compares stdout byte-for-byte.\nfunc TestDuRecursiveTraversal(t *testing.T) {\n\tt.Parallel()\n\n\tdir := t.TempDir()\n\tbuildDirTree(t, dir)\n\n\ttestutils.RunDiffTests(t, goBinary, refBinary, []testutils.DiffTest{\n\t\t{\n\t\t\tName:     \"recursive-directory-tree\",\n\t\t\tArgs:     []string{dir},\n\t\t\tExitCode: 0,\n\t\t},\n\t})\n}\n\n// TestDuBlockUnits verifies R1.2: both binaries report identical block counts\n// for the same directory tree, confirming the Go implementation's 512-to-1024\n// block conversion matches the reference binary. The byte-for-byte stdout\n// comparison in RunDiffTests confirms block counts agree.\nfunc TestDuBlockUnits(t *testing.T) {\n\tt.Parallel()\n\n\tdir := t.TempDir()\n\tbuildDirTree(t, dir)\n\n\t// Run on a subdirectory to verify block counts at a different tree level.\n\tsub := filepath.Join(dir, \"sub\")\n\n\ttestutils.RunDiffTests(t, goBinary, refBinary, []testutils.DiffTest{\n\t\t{\n\t\t\tName:     \"block-units-subdirectory\",\n\t\t\tArgs:     []string{sub},\n\t\t\tExitCode: 0,\n\t\t},\n\t})\n}\n\n// TestDuOutputFormat verifies R1.3: each output line is \"SIZE\\tPATH\\n\" for both\n// directory and non-directory file arguments.\n// D4: tests both directory and non-directory arguments to exercise both branches\n// of duArg.\nfunc TestDuOutputFormat(t *testing.T) {\n\tt.Parallel()\n\n\tdir := t.TempDir()\n\tbuildDirTree(t, dir)\n\n\tfilePath := filepath.Join(dir, \"file1.txt\")\n\n\ttestutils.RunDiffTests(t, goBinary, refBinary, []testutils.DiffTest{\n\t\t{\n\t\t\tName:     \"format-directory-arg\",\n\t\t\tArgs:     []string{dir},\n\t\t\tExitCode: 0,\n\t\t},\n\t\t{\n\t\t\tName:     \"format-non-directory-arg\",\n\t\t\tArgs:     []string{filePath},\n\t\t\tExitCode: 0,\n\t\t},\n\t})\n}\n\n// TestDuDefaultArgument verifies R1.5: when no arguments are given, du defaults\n// to \".\". Run both binaries with no arguments inside a temporary directory and\n// verify identical output.\nfunc TestDuDefaultArgument(t *testing.T) {\n\tt.Parallel()\n\n\tdir := t.TempDir()\n\tbuildDirTree(t, dir)\n\n\ttestutils.RunDiffTests(t, goBinary, refBinary, []testutils.DiffTest{\n\t\t{\n\t\t\tName:     \"default-current-directory\",\n\t\t\tWorkDir:  dir,\n\t\t\tExitCode: 0,\n\t\t},\n\t})\n}\n\n// TestDuMultipleArguments verifies R1.5: multiple directory arguments are\n// processed in command-line order, each traversed independently.\nfunc TestDuMultipleArguments(t *testing.T) {\n\tt.Parallel()\n\n\tdir := t.TempDir()\n\tbuildDirTree(t, dir)\n\n\tsub := filepath.Join(dir, \"sub\")\n\tdeep := filepath.Join(dir, \"sub\", \"deep\")\n\n\ttestutils.RunDiffTests(t, goBinary, refBinary, []testutils.DiffTest{\n\t\t{\n\t\t\tName:     \"multiple-directory-args\",\n\t\t\tArgs:     []string{sub, deep},\n\t\t\tExitCode: 0,\n\t\t},\n\t})\n}\n\n// TestDuNonexistentArgument verifies R4.1 and R4.2: du exits 1 for a\n// nonexistent path and prints a diagnostic to stderr.\nfunc TestDuNonexistentArgument(t *testing.T) {\n\tt.Parallel()\n\n\tdir := t.TempDir()\n\n\ttestutils.RunDiffTests(t, goBinary, refBinary, []testutils.DiffTest{\n\t\t{\n\t\t\tName:      \"nonexistent-path\",\n\t\t\tArgs:      []string{filepath.Join(dir, \"does-not-exist\")},\n\t\t\tExitCode:  1,\n\t\t\tNormalize: []testutils.NormalizeFunc{normalizeProgramName},\n\t\t},\n\t})\n}\n","numLines":214,"startLine":1,"totalLines":214}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013JkZSbgGWB15gGzjWN9aDT","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: test-rel01.3\n     2→title: \"Test Suite: rel01.3 — du specification and differential test\"\n     3→release: rel01.3\n     4→\n     5→traces:\n     6→  - rel01.3-uc001-du\n     7→  - prd009-du R1\n     8→  - prd009-du R2\n     9→  - prd009-du R3\n    10→  - prd009-du R4\n    11→  - prd009-du R5\n    12→\n    13→preconditions:\n    14→  - Binaries exist under ./bin/ (built via mage build).\n    15→  - Reference binaries are installed via Homebrew gdu (coreutils).\n    16→  - Tests are run via go test ./cmd/du/... which invokes RunDiffTests from pkg/testutils.\n    17→  - All tests use LC_ALL=C to eliminate locale-dependent divergence.\n    18→  - No normalization is applied; du output is deterministic when both binaries run on the same directory on the same filesystem.\n    19→  - All block-count tests use -k to pin output to 1024-byte blocks and eliminate platform default divergence.\n    20→  - Tests use a fixture directory (testdata/du-fixture/) containing nested files of known sizes, a second subdirectory for multi-argument tests, and files of varied sizes.\n    21→  - Hard-link pairs are created in test setup (not committed) to test dedup. All files in the fixture are on the same filesystem as the test runner.\n    22→\n    23→test_cases:\n    24→  - name: du_default_recursive\n    25→    description: Default flags with -k on fixture directory; one line per subdirectory and root.\n    26→    inputs:\n    27→      args: [\"-k\", \"testdata/du-fixture/\"]\n    28→      stdin: null\n    29→      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    30→    normalization: none\n    31→    expected:\n    32→      exit_code: 0\n    33→      stdout_structure: one \"SIZE\\tPATH\" line per subdirectory, ending with the root directory total\n    34→      stderr: \"\"\n    35→    traces:\n    36→      - prd009-du R1.1\n    37→      - prd009-du R1.2\n    38→      - prd009-du R1.3\n    39→\n    40→  - name: du_summary_mode\n    41→    description: -s prints only the total for each argument.\n    42→    inputs:\n    43→      args: [\"-sk\", \"testdata/du-fixture/\"]\n    44→      stdin: null\n    45→      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    46→    normalization: none\n    47→    expected:\n    48→      exit_code: 0\n    49→      stdout_structure: one line with total size and path\n    50→      stderr: \"\"\n    51→    traces:\n    52→      - prd009-du R2.2\n    53→\n    54→  - name: du_all_files\n    55→    description: -a prints size for every file, not just directories.\n    56→    inputs:\n    57→      args: [\"-ak\", \"testdata/du-fixture/\"]\n    58→      stdin: null\n    59→      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    60→    normalization: none\n    61→    expected:\n    62→      exit_code: 0\n    63→      stdout_structure: one line per file and directory in the tree\n    64→      stderr: \"\"\n    65→    traces:\n    66→      - prd009-du R2.3\n    67→\n    68→  - name: du_human_readable\n    69→    description: -h displays sizes as human-readable strings (K/M/G).\n    70→    inputs:\n    71→      args: [\"-h\", \"testdata/du-fixture/\"]\n    72→      stdin: null\n    73→      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    74→    normalization: none\n    75→    expected:\n    76→      exit_code: 0\n    77→      stdout_structure: size values contain unit suffixes (K, M, G)\n    78→      stderr: \"\"\n    79→    traces:\n    80→      - prd009-du R2.1\n    81→\n    82→  - name: du_max_depth_1\n    83→    description: -d 1 limits output to the argument and its immediate children.\n    84→    inputs:\n    85→      args: [\"-k\", \"-d\", \"1\", \"testdata/du-fixture/\"]\n    86→      stdin: null\n    87→      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    88→    normalization: none\n    89→    expected:\n    90→      exit_code: 0\n    91→      stdout_structure: lines for immediate subdirectories and the root; no deeper entries\n    92→      stderr: \"\"\n    93→    traces:\n    94→      - prd009-du R2.4\n    95→\n    96→  - name: du_grand_total\n    97→    description: -c prints a grand total \"total\" line after all arguments.\n    98→    inputs:\n    99→      args: [\"-ck\", \"testdata/du-fixture/subdir1\", \"testdata/du-fixture/subdir2\"]\n   100→      stdin: null\n   101→      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n   102→    normalization: none\n   103→    expected:\n   104→      exit_code: 0\n   105→      stdout_structure: per-argument lines followed by a \"SIZE\\ttotal\" line\n   106→      stderr: \"\"\n   107→    traces:\n   108→      - prd009-du R2.7\n   109→\n   110→  - name: du_hard_link_dedup\n   111→    description: 'A file hard-linked into two directories is counted only once. Setup: create testdata/du-hardlink-fixture/ with dir_a/shared.txt (a regular file) and dir_b/shared.txt (hard link to dir_a/shared.txt, same inode). The hard link is created at test time via os.Link.'\n   112→    inputs:\n   113→      args: [\"-k\", \"testdata/du-hardlink-fixture/\"]\n   114→      stdin: null\n   115→      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n   116→    normalization: none\n   117→    expected:\n   118→      exit_code: 0\n   119→      stdout_structure: total for the root counts shared.txt blocks once, not twice\n   120→      stderr: \"\"\n   121→    traces:\n   122→      - prd009-du R3.1\n   123→      - prd009-du R3.2\n   124→      - prd009-du R3.3\n   125→\n   126→  - name: du_apparent_size\n   127→    description: --apparent-size reports st_size instead of st_blocks.\n   128→    inputs:\n   129→      args: [\"--apparent-size\", \"-k\", \"testdata/du-fixture/\"]\n   130→      stdin: null\n   131→      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n   132→    normalization: none\n   133→    expected:\n   134→      exit_code: 0\n   135→      stdout_structure: sizes based on file content length, not block allocation\n   136→      stderr: \"\"\n   137→    traces:\n   138→      - prd009-du R2.8\n   139→\n   140→  - name: du_kilobyte_units\n   141→    description: -k reports in 1024-byte blocks (default, accepted without error).\n   142→    inputs:\n   143→      args: [\"-k\", \"testdata/du-fixture/\"]\n   144→      stdin: null\n   145→      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n   146→    normalization: none\n   147→    expected:\n   148→      exit_code: 0\n   149→      stdout_structure: identical to default_recursive (1K is the default)\n   150→      stderr: \"\"\n   151→    traces:\n   152→      - prd009-du R2.5\n   153→\n   154→  - name: du_megabyte_units\n   155→    description: -m reports in 1048576-byte (1M) blocks.\n   156→    inputs:\n   157→      args: [\"-m\", \"testdata/du-fixture/\"]\n   158→      stdin: null\n   159→      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n   160→    normalization: none\n   161→    expected:\n   162→      exit_code: 0\n   163→      stdout_structure: sizes in megabyte units, smaller values show as 1 (rounded up)\n   164→      stderr: \"\"\n   165→    traces:\n   166→      - prd009-du R2.6\n   167→\n   168→  - name: du_missing_path\n   169→    description: Non-existent argument prints error to stderr, exits 1.\n   170→    inputs:\n   171→      args: [\"-k\", \"nonexistent_dir\"]\n   172→      stdin: null\n   173→      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n   174→    normalization: none\n   175→    expected:\n   176→      exit_code: 0\n   177→      stderr_contains: \"nonexistent_dir\"\n   178→    traces:\n   179→      - prd009-du R4.2\n   180→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"afee3052-baa4-4d89-84b3-61d45e69f953","uuid":"654de337-08fe-417c-a4c1-6119c42ff145","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/239/docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel01.3.yaml","content":"id: test-rel01.3\ntitle: \"Test Suite: rel01.3 — du specification and differential test\"\nrelease: rel01.3\n\ntraces:\n  - rel01.3-uc001-du\n  - prd009-du R1\n  - prd009-du R2\n  - prd009-du R3\n  - prd009-du R4\n  - prd009-du R5\n\npreconditions:\n  - Binaries exist under ./bin/ (built via mage build).\n  - Reference binaries are installed via Homebrew gdu (coreutils).\n  - Tests are run via go test ./cmd/du/... which invokes RunDiffTests from pkg/testutils.\n  - All tests use LC_ALL=C to eliminate locale-dependent divergence.\n  - No normalization is applied; du output is deterministic when both binaries run on the same directory on the same filesystem.\n  - All block-count tests use -k to pin output to 1024-byte blocks and eliminate platform default divergence.\n  - Tests use a fixture directory (testdata/du-fixture/) containing nested files of known sizes, a second subdirectory for multi-argument tests, and files of varied sizes.\n  - Hard-link pairs are created in test setup (not committed) to test dedup. All files in the fixture are on the same filesystem as the test runner.\n\ntest_cases:\n  - name: du_default_recursive\n    description: Default flags with -k on fixture directory; one line per subdirectory and root.\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"-k\", \"testdata/du-fixture/\"]\n      stdin: null\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: one \"SIZE\\tPATH\" line per subdirectory, ending with the root directory total\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd009-du R1.1\n      - prd009-du R1.2\n      - prd009-du R1.3\n\n  - name: du_summary_mode\n    description: -s prints only the total for each argument.\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"-sk\", \"testdata/du-fixture/\"]\n      stdin: null\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: one line with total size and path\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd009-du R2.2\n\n  - name: du_all_files\n    description: -a prints size for every file, not just directories.\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"-ak\", \"testdata/du-fixture/\"]\n      stdin: null\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: one line per file and directory in the tree\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd009-du R2.3\n\n  - name: du_human_readable\n    description: -h displays sizes as human-readable strings (K/M/G).\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"-h\", \"testdata/du-fixture/\"]\n      stdin: null\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: size values contain unit suffixes (K, M, G)\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd009-du R2.1\n\n  - name: du_max_depth_1\n    description: -d 1 limits output to the argument and its immediate children.\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"-k\", \"-d\", \"1\", \"testdata/du-fixture/\"]\n      stdin: null\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: lines for immediate subdirectories and the root; no deeper entries\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd009-du R2.4\n\n  - name: du_grand_total\n    description: -c prints a grand total \"total\" line after all arguments.\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"-ck\", \"testdata/du-fixture/subdir1\", \"testdata/du-fixture/subdir2\"]\n      stdin: null\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: per-argument lines followed by a \"SIZE\\ttotal\" line\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd009-du R2.7\n\n  - name: du_hard_link_dedup\n    description: 'A file hard-linked into two directories is counted only once. Setup: create testdata/du-hardlink-fixture/ with dir_a/shared.txt (a regular file) and dir_b/shared.txt (hard link to dir_a/shared.txt, same inode). The hard link is created at test time via os.Link.'\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"-k\", \"testdata/du-hardlink-fixture/\"]\n      stdin: null\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: total for the root counts shared.txt blocks once, not twice\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd009-du R3.1\n      - prd009-du R3.2\n      - prd009-du R3.3\n\n  - name: du_apparent_size\n    description: --apparent-size reports st_size instead of st_blocks.\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"--apparent-size\", \"-k\", \"testdata/du-fixture/\"]\n      stdin: null\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: sizes based on file content length, not block allocation\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd009-du R2.8\n\n  - name: du_kilobyte_units\n    description: -k reports in 1024-byte blocks (default, accepted without error).\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"-k\", \"testdata/du-fixture/\"]\n      stdin: null\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: identical to default_recursive (1K is the default)\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd009-du R2.5\n\n  - name: du_megabyte_units\n    description: -m reports in 1048576-byte (1M) blocks.\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"-m\", \"testdata/du-fixture/\"]\n      stdin: null\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: sizes in megabyte units, smaller values show as 1 (rounded up)\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd009-du R2.6\n\n  - name: du_missing_path\n    description: Non-existent argument prints error to stderr, exits 1.\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"-k\", \"nonexistent_dir\"]\n      stdin: null\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stderr_contains: \"nonexistent_dir\"\n    traces:\n      - prd009-du R4.2\n","numLines":180,"startLine":1,"totalLines":180}}}
